<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The catalogs are shipped netstandard2.0 libraries, so this joins the .NET Framework 4.7.2
    floor. What it checks is read out of their metadata by reflection, which is the same surface
    the analyzers read across an assembly boundary — running it on the real .NET Framework CLR is
    therefore not ceremony.
  -->
  <Import Project="..\..\build\Net472TestFloor.props" />

  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>false</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      SYSLIB1045 (use [GeneratedRegex]) cannot be honoured here. The regex source generator needs
      .NET 7 or later, and this project also builds for net472 — that is the whole point of the
      floor it imports above. A partial method carrying the attribute would simply not compile on
      the inner build that matters most. The sibling CatalogGen.UnitTests targets net10.0 only and
      does use [GeneratedRegex]; this waiver is about the floor, not about the rule.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);SYSLIB1045</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The generated sources are copied beside the test binary because one invariant cannot be seen
    from metadata: whether a rule reached its category through the shared constant or repeated the
    literal. Constant folding erases the difference — both produce the same value — so that check
    has to read the source. Copying rather than resolving a path at run time keeps it working under
    the deterministic CI build, which rewrites compile-time source paths.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\src\**\*.g.cs" Link="catalogs\%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The consumer-facing documents come along for the same reason, one level up: which upstream
    release a catalogue mirrors is the first thing a consumer needs, and nothing compiles a README,
    so a banner left behind by a regeneration would be wrong and silent. DocumentedMirrorTests reads
    these against the CatalogSource attribute the generator wrote next to them. The project folder is
    preserved in the link, because that is what ties a README to its catalogue.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\src\**\README.md;..\..\src\**\CHANGELOG.md"
             Link="catalogdocs\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Two registries come along, because DocumentedSiblingsTests asks two different questions of them.
    Which catalogues exist is eng/catalogs.json, the manifest the generator and the nightly workflow
    already read — a catalogue enters it before it exists. What this repository PUBLISHES is the
    <ReleaseTrain> declaration a project carries in its own .csproj, which is what makes it packable
    and "the one file that cannot be forgotten" (tools/trains.sh); the analyzers and the foundation
    are published without being catalogues. The repository README joins them as the one document
    that has to name the catalogues from outside them.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\eng\catalogs.json" Link="catalogmanifest\catalogs.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!--
      Copied as project.xml, NOT as a .csproj: tools/trains.sh discovers what a train publishes by
      grepping the whole tree for <ReleaseTrain> in *.csproj, and a project file sitting in a build
      output is indistinguishable from a real one. Copied under its own name, each of these was
      found and handed to dotnet pack, which failed on it for want of a restore. The folder still
      names the project, which is all the tests read it for.
    -->
    <Content Include="..\..\src\**\*.csproj"
             Link="catalogprojects\%(RecursiveDir)project.xml" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="..\..\README.md" Link="repodocs\README.md" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package icons, so PackageIconTests can compare them. bin/ and obj/ are excluded for the
    reason the generated sources are: a build output holds copies, and an icon compared against
    its own copy is a catalogue that looks duplicated.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="..\..\src\**\icon.png"
             Exclude="..\..\src\**\bin\**\icon.png;..\..\src\**\obj\**\icon.png"
             Link="catalogicons\%(RecursiveDir)icon.png" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="..\..\icon.png" Link="repoicon\icon.png" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The foundation is NOT referenced here. Each catalogue now depends on the published package, so
    referencing the project as well puts two DiagnosticCatalog assemblies in front of the compiler —
    0.0.0.0 from the project, 0.1.0.0 from the package — and MSB3243 resolves the conflict
    "arbitrarily", which the CI warning ratchet turns into an error.

    Taking it transitively is also the more honest shape: this project asserts what a CONSUMER of a
    catalogue sees, and a consumer gets the foundation exactly this way. DiagnosticCatalog.UnitTests
    keeps its project reference, because the foundation itself is what that one tests.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.Sonar\DiagnosticCatalog.Sonar.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.NetAnalyzers\DiagnosticCatalog.NetAnalyzers.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.StyleCop\DiagnosticCatalog.StyleCop.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.CodeStyle\DiagnosticCatalog.CodeStyle.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.Xunit\DiagnosticCatalog.Xunit.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DiagnosticCatalog.NUnit\DiagnosticCatalog.NUnit.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
